faq:
  - q: Is attachment formed only with the mother?
    a: No. Attachment describes the close emotional bond a baby forms with the people who care for them. That person can be the mother, but also the father, a grandparent or another adult who regularly looks after the baby.
  - q: What does secure attachment look like?
    a: A securely attached child acts with confidence and explores their surroundings while the parent is nearby. They may be a little upset when the parent leaves, and when the parent returns they quickly reconnect and settle.
  - q: Do I need to respond to every cry straight away?
    a: The bond grows from the overall pattern of the relationship, not from single moments. What matters is that the baby's signals are, most of the time, met with attention and in a consistent way. Not being able to respond instantly every time is an ordinary part of parenting.
  - q: Who developed attachment theory?
    a: Attachment theory was developed by the British psychiatrist and psychoanalyst John Bowlby. It was later expanded through the work of Mary Ainsworth.
  - q: Where can I get support with attachment?
    a: If you would like to talk about your relationship with your child or about your own tiredness, parent counselling offers a space for that. Sessions last 50 minutes and can take place in Turkish or English, at our centre in Famagusta or online.
