blocks:
  - type: metin
    baslik: What attachment means
    metin: |-
      The American Psychological Association (APA) Dictionary of Psychology defines attachment theory in two parts. First, the need of the young to stay close to their caregivers and to form close emotional bonds with them. Second, the different kinds of relationship that form between infants and caregivers. According to the dictionary, these relationships affect the individual's later emotional development. The theory was developed by John Bowlby and later expanded by Mary Ainsworth ([APA](https://dictionary.apa.org/attachment-theory)).

      One point in this definition stands out: attachment is not a trait the child carries alone. It describes the relationship between the child and the adult who cares for them. So rather than saying "my child isn't well attached", it is more useful to ask "how is the bond between us working?"

      Bowlby's work is also part of psychodynamic thinking. If you are curious about that link, see our article [what is psychodynamic psychotherapy](/en/blog/what-is-psychodynamic-psychotherapy.html).
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: What does secure attachment look like?
    giris: 'The same dictionary describes secure attachment through how a child behaves during a brief separation from the parent and the reunion that follows ([APA](https://dictionary.apa.org/secure-attachment)):'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: While the parent is there
        metin: The child acts with confidence. They reach for toys, explore their surroundings with curiosity and look back at the parent now and then.
      - baslik: When the parent leaves
        metin: They show mild distress; they may cry or look towards the door. This reaction is ordinary. We describe when moments of separation are to be expected in our article on [separation anxiety in children](/en/blog/anxiety-and-separation-anxiety-in-children.html).
      - baslik: When the parent returns
        metin: The child quickly reconnects. They hug the parent, turn towards them, settle within a short time and can go back to playing.
  - type: metin
    metin: |-
      This description is not a checklist. How a child reacts on a day when they are tired or unwell does not, on its own, say much about the bond. The bond shows in the overall pattern of the relationship rather than in single moments.
  - type: metin
    baslik: How is the bond built? Serve and return
    metin: |-
      A bond is woven from small, repeated exchanges more than from big moments. The Center on the Developing Child at Harvard University calls these exchanges *serve and return*. When a baby babbles, points at something or cries, the adult responds with eye contact, words or a hug. This back-and-forth goes to and fro like a game of tennis. According to the Center, these interactions strengthen the brain circuits at the core of a child's early emotional well-being and social skills ([Center on the Developing Child](https://developingchild.harvard.edu/key-concepts/serve-and-return/)).

      The same source makes one more point: naming what a child is seeing, doing or feeling helps build language connections, even before the child can talk.
  - type: onayli-liste
    baslik: Serve and return in everyday life
    giris: 'This does not need a special toy or set-aside time. Everyday moments are enough:'
    maddeler:
      - baslik: Look at what the baby is looking at
        aciklama: If they point at a bird or a light, follow their interest and name what they are seeing.
      - baslik: Respond to crying with your voice and your touch
        aciklama: 'A short sentence such as "You''re hungry, aren''t you?" puts a name to what they are feeling.'
      - baslik: Talk during feeding, bath time and dressing
        aciklama: Describing what you are doing turns routine moments into back-and-forth exchanges too.
      - baslik: Leave the next turn to the baby
        aciklama: When they make a sound or smile, respond, then wait for them to "talk" again.
  - type: metin
    baslik: Parents may need support too
    metin: |-
      This does not mean every cry needs an instant response. The point the same source stresses is when an adult's responses are *persistently* inconsistent, harmful or absent ([Center on the Developing Child](https://developingchild.harvard.edu/key-concepts/serve-and-return/)). A parent who is sometimes tired, distracted or slow to respond is simply going through ordinary parenthood.

      The source also notes that heavy stress on a parent, such as money worries, isolation or ongoing health problems, can make responsive caregiving harder. So one way to support a child is to support the adult who cares for them.
  - type: metin
    baslik: When can you seek support?
    metin: |-
      If something about your relationship with your child is on your mind, or your own tiredness is making it harder to respond to your baby, you do not need to wait for a problem to grow before talking about it. Frequent [tantrums](/en/blog/tantrums-and-anger-in-children.html), difficulties around separation or [difficult times in the family](/en/blog/family-transitions-parent-counselling.html) are also worth talking about.

      In [parent counselling](/en/services/parent-counselling.html) we look at your child's behaviour and their relationship with you together. When it makes sense to work with the child directly, our [child psychology](/en/services/child-psychology.html) page explains how we go about it. Sessions can take place in Turkish or English, at our centre in Famagusta or [online](/en/services/online-therapy.html). If you have questions, [get in touch](/en/contact.html).
